        vm.assume(initialOwner != address(0));
        vm.assume(newOwner != address(0));
        vm.assume(initialOwner != newOwner);

        // fund and impersonate initialOwner so it can call transferOwnership
        vm.deal(initialOwner, 1 ether);
        // set contract owner to initialOwner by using transferOwnership: but onlyOwner restricts calls until owner is set.
        // The deployed contract's owner is zero address by default. To set owner to initialOwner, we need to bypass onlyOwner.
        // However, in the provided C, owner is public but uninitialized; we can only call transferOwnership as owner.
        // To make initialOwner the current owner for the test, we exploit that owner is initially address(0) and onlyOwner requires msg.sender == owner.
        // So we must set owner to initialOwner by deploying a new C and then directly setting via calling transferOwnership from address(0) is impossible.
        // Instead, we will assume the initial owner is address(this) (the test contract) to be able to call transferOwnership; but tests must keep fuzzing parameters.
